ad. But previous experiments likely modified body_force directly with `#pragma omp parallel for` inside it (creating a new parallel region each call). That was baseline? Actually original had no OpenMP; they added pragmas. The best experiment 9adc used \"one OpenMP parallel region per iteration\" and combined force+update, so likely inlined.\n\nGiven we want to propose something new, let's inline for clarity and control scheduling.\n\nWe'll produce a modified src/nbody.c that:\n\n- Includes <omp.h>? Not needed.\n- In main loop, uses `#pragma omp parallel` with two worksharing loops: first guided on force/velocity update, second static on position update.\n\nLet's write the code exactly. We'll keep comments and structure similar to original but add pragmas.\n\nWe must ensure that variables like dt are shared; they are constants so fine. `bodies` is pointer shared.\n\nOne subtlety: The inner loop uses p[i] which changes per i, but each thread has its own private i, so no race. Good.\n\nLet's produce the content.\n\nAlso note: We should not change anything else like read/write functions.\n\nNow we need to output JSON with hypothesis and transformations.\n\nHypothesis: \"Using a guided schedule for the force-computation outer loop inside a per-iteration OpenMP parallel region (with static-scheduled position update) may reduce scheduling overhead compared to dynamic chunk=16 while preserving load balance, potentially improving execution time without changing floating-point accumulation order.\"\n\nTransformations list: [\"Add #pragma omp parallel around each iteration with guided schedule on force loop and static schedule on position update loop\"].\n\nSources_kept: [] because integration_source_ids empty.\n\nNotes: mention that we preserve exact output by keeping per-body inner-loop accumulation sequential, and use one parallel region per iteration to reduce thread creation overhead.\n\nLet's craft the file content. We'll include the full source with modifications.\n\nWe need to be careful about indentation and comments.\n\nI'll write:\n\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <math.h>\n\n#define N_ITER 10 // number of simulation iterations\n#define DT 0.01f // time step\n#define SOFTENING 1e-9f // to avoid zero divisors\n\n/*\n * Each body holds coordinate positions (i.e., x, y, and z) and\n * velocities (i.e., vx, vy, and vz).\n */\ntypedef struct { float x, y, z, vx, vy, vz; } Body;\n\n/*\n * Compute the gravitational impact among all pairs of bodies in \n * the system.\n */\nvoid body_force(Body *p, float dt, int n) {\n  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n    float fx = 0.0f; \n    float fy = 0.0f; \n    float fz = 0.0f;\n\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n      float dx = p[j].x - p[i].x;\n      float dy = p[j].y - p[i].y;\n      float dz = p[j].z - p[i].z;\n      float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\n      float inv_dist = 1 / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\n      float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\n\n      fx += dx * inv_dist3; \n      fy += dy * inv_dist3; \n      fz += dz * inv_dist3;\n    }\n\n    p[i].vx += dt*fx; \n    p[i].vy += dt*fy; \n    p[i].vz += dt*fz;\n  }\n}\n\n/*\n * Read a binary dataset with initilized bodies.\n */\nBody* read_dataset(int *nbodies) {\n  int b = fread(nbodies, sizeof(*nbodies), 1, stdin);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\nError reading nbodie value\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n  Body *bodies = (Body *)malloc(*nbodies * sizeof(Body));\n  b = fread(bodies, *nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdin);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\nError reading input values\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n\n  return bodies;\n}\n\n/*\n * Write simulation results into a binary dataset.\n */\nvoid write_dataset(const int nbodies, Body *bodies) {\n  int b = fwrite(bodies, nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdout);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\nError writing to output\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n}\n\nint main(int argc,char **argv) {\n  int nbodies;\n\n  Body *bodies = read_dataset(&nbodies);\n\n  /*\n   * At each simulation iteration, interbody forces are computed,\n   * and bodies' positions are integrated.\n   */\n  for (int iter = 0; iter < N_ITER; iter++) {\n    #pragma omp parallel\n    {\n      #pragma omp for schedule(guided)\n      for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n        float fx = 0.0f;\n        float fy = 0.0f;\n        float fz = 0.0f;\n\n        for (int j = 0; j < nbodies; j++) {\n          float dx = bodies[j].x - bodies[i].x;\n          float dy = bodies[j].y - bodies[i].y;\n          float dz = bodies[j].z - bodies[i].z;\n          float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\n          float inv_dist = 1 / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\n          float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\n\n          fx += dx * inv_dist3;\n          fy += dy * inv_dist3;\n          fz += dz * inv_dist3;\n        }\n\n        bodies[i].vx += DT*fx;\n        bodies[i].vy += DT*fy;\n        bodies[i].vz += DT*fz;\n      }\n      #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n      for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n        bodies[i].x += bodies[i].vx * DT;\n        bodies[i].y += bodies[i].vy * DT;\n        bodies[i].z += bodies[i].vz * DT;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  write_dataset(nbodies, bodies);\n\n  free(bodies);\n  \n  exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);\n}\n\nWe removed the body_force function? Actually we kept it but not used. That's fine; compiler may warn unused function but no error. We could remove it to avoid warnings, but better keep for minimal changes? The instructions say \"Edit only source files under src/.\" and preserve other experiments/unrelated code. Removing an unused function is okay but might affect build if there are references elsewhere? Only this file. But we can leave it; it's harmless.\n\nHowever note that the original body_force had a bug: it used `p[i].vx += dt*fx` etc, which updates velocities. Our inlined version does same. Good.\n\nWe must ensure no other changes like variable 